[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 3. Out-of-range conversion: clamp in `long`, then truncate to `int`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is the subtlest agreement. glibc's `%d` converts through a `long`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m saturating at `LONG_MAX` / `LONG_MIN` on overflow, and only then assigns to the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `int*`, truncating. So:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | input | glibc `long` | stored `int` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `9223372036854775807` and up | `LONG_MAX` | `-1` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-9223372036854775808` and below | `LONG_MIN` | `0` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `Scanner::scan_i32` reproduces this by accumulating into `i64` with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `saturating_mul`/`saturating_add` and then casting `as i32`. Saturating at each
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m step and clamping once at the end coincide here because both land on exactly
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `i64::MAX` / `i64::MIN`. A naive translation using `i32` accumulation, or
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `str::parse::<i32>()` with a fallback, would give `0`, `i32::MAX`, or a parse
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m error instead of `-1`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Tests: `out_of_range_conversion_is_clamped_then_truncated`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `very_long_digit_strings` (4000-digit inputs).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
